    thinking: "**Clarifying My Understanding**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! It\'s now perfectly clear that the input text IS the prompt itself. This simplifies everything significantly. I\'m focusing intently on the current environment: I\'m in the AI Studio React + Vite + Express workspace, and the template uses express, tsx, react, and react-dom.\n\n\n**Evaluating Architecture Options**\n\nI\'m now weighing the best architecture for this project within the AI Studio Build environment. I\'m focusing on the interplay between the framework\'s recommendations (Express/TypeScript/Vite) and the user\'s preferred technologies (Python/FastAPI). The constraints require a server on port 3000. Considering this, I am trying to determine if I can run a clean Express+SQLite backend, or if I should check for Python dependencies. The goal is to choose the most streamlined solution for development and deployment in this specific workspace. I\'m prioritizing compatibility and ease of integration.\n\n\n**Confirming Optimal Implementation**\n\nI\'ve determined that leveraging the existing `server.ts` with Express and `better-sqlite3` offers the most robust solution. This setup allows for a fully persistent, transactional SQLite backend with JWT authentication and bcrypt password hashing. It works natively within AI Studio\'s preview, development (with `restart_dev_server`), and production deployment via `npm run build` and `npm start`. This avoids any complexities of managing separate Python processes. I\'m noting the initial preference for Python, FastAPI, etc., aligns more with pre-existing Python codebases, while our template uses Express as a standard.\n\n\n**Defining Package Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now outlining necessary packages, in order. For the front end, `react-router-dom` is a must for routing, along with `axios` for API calls with a JWT interceptor. I\'m also planning to use `recharts` for analytics, and `leaflet`, `react-leaflet`, and `@types/leaflet` for an interactive map. I need `qrcode.react` for parcel identities. For the backend, I\'ll use `better-sqlite3` for the database, and `@types/better-sqlite3`, `jsonwebtoken`, `@types/jsonwebtoken`, and `bcryptjs` for security.\n\n\n**Refining Dependencies and React**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on package details. I\'ll need `@types/bcryptjs` for secure password hashing and `multer` & `@types/multer` for parcel photograph uploads. I\'m investigating React 19 compatibility, which is crucial. I\'ve confirmed that `react` and `react-dom` are both at version `^19.0.1`. It\'s a key consideration for `react-leaflet`. I\'m going to determine if `leaflet` directly in a clean React component provides more deterministic map control with React 19.\n\n\n**Addressing Map Interaction**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on map interaction. I\'ve pinpointed key caching bugs related to stale location values as described earlier! I\'m now implementing `map.fitBounds(...)` for endpoint changes, interactive map pin selection, and custom SVG pins. I will use a simulated live GPS parcel marker, and test our map polyline. I\'m verifying React 19 compatibility with `leaflet`, `@types/leaflet`.\n\n\n**Specifying Style Guide**\n\nI\'m now detailing the style guide. I\'ve defined the primary colors: `#101114`, `#FFFFFF`, `#FFD43B`, `#F5F5F5`, and `#25282D`. I\'ve outlined a crisp dark text on yellow `#FFD43B` accent for AAA contrast. I\'m choosing `\'Plus Jakarta Sans\'`, `\'DM Sans\'`, and `\'JetBrains Mono\'` for typography. I\'m focusing on strict adherence to the zero-pill and anti-slop rules.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Visual Design**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the mobile bottom tab bar design; it must be under 15% of viewport height with a clean elevation. I\'m also considering the use of bespoke visual assets versus vector iconography. I\'m leaning towards clean vector icons and interactive OpenStreetMap, Rapido-inspired, because it shines. I\'m checking if 2 editorial images for the public landing are an upgrade, without cluttering the opening screen.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on UI details for the opening screen. I\'m prioritizing a simple, uncluttered design with custom SVG mobility graphics from Lucide. I want users to be able to immediately choose between \"Send a Parcel\" or \"Deliver a Parcel\" without visual distractions. I\'m verifying the prompt\'s requirements for activity selection and consider incorporating a sample photo option.\n\n\n**Defining User Roles**\n\nI\'m now detailing user roles and authentication. I\'m building out sender and partner mode selection with partner verification, considering quick onboarding. Public pages like \'How It Works\' and \'Safety\' require clean navigation. I\'m focusing on user management: registration, login, logout, and robust password validation. I will implement demo accounts for testing.\n\n\n**Implementing Geocoding and Routing**\n\nI\'m now implementing the critical map bug fix and geocoding/routing as discussed. I\'m focusing on explicit, separate state variables for pickup and destination coordinates. I\'m building a real OpenStreetMap Nominatim geocoding solution, including local fallback dictionaries for rapid resolution of cities like Goa and other major Indian locations. I\'m setting up a backend API proxy for `/api/geocode` and `/api/route` calls to prevent race conditions. I will use the OSRM public routing API with a Haversine road-factor fallback.\n\n\n**Developing Geocoding and Location**\n\nI\'m working on geolocation. I\'m building out options for users to determine locations, including automatic GPS, manual map selection, and autocomplete with a Nominatim geocoder. I\'m now integrating OpenStreetMap API calls. I am adding error handling and presets for quick testing, including Panaji, Mumbai, and Goa.\n\n\n\n**Calculating Parcel Pricing**\n\nI\'m now calculating parcel pricing. The pricing formula is: Final Price = Base Fare + Distance Charge + Weight Charge + Dimension Charge + Delivery Model Charge + Urgency Charge + Platform Fee. I\'m focusing on mode-specific rates. Considering bikes for local/short, cars for slightly more, trains and buses for intercity and long-distance options, and commercial freight for larger parcels.\n\n\n**Designing Booking Interface**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface for parcel booking! I\'m creating the **MOVEX ESTIMATED FARE** breakdown card to match the backend calculations exactly. Now, I\'m integrating the 6-step booking flow, including navigation, dashboard, and the steps for pickup, destination, delivery model, and transportation mode. I\'m focusing on making it intuitive!\n\n\n\n**Detailing Booking Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing step 4 and 5 in booking. I\'m focusing on parcel info: category, weight, dimensions, declared contents, photo upload, declared value, and fragility. I\'m also implementing the MOVEX Guard eligibility check. For step 5, I\'m integrating the partner matching engine and smart meeting point recommendations, which will determine community relay.\n\n\n**Finalizing Booking Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing the final steps of booking. I am integrating route compatibility, partner reliability, ETA and charges, and AI-suggested meeting points. I\'m focusing on the sender-partner selection for pickup and drop-off, or doorstep delivery for premium service. I\'m then implementing a broadcast option for wide partner reach. I am making it all intuitive!\n\n\n**Defining Journey Publication**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Publish Journey\" feature for partners. I\'m focusing on the overview stats: earnings, deliveries, and ratings. I will implement a publish journey feature with starting location, destination, travel details, transportation mode, parcel capacity, and weight limits. I\'m adding a mandatory declaration, and CRUD for recurring schedules. I\'m also preparing for the parcel requests feed.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n\n**Implementing Partner Interaction**\n\nI\'m now detailing partner request handling. My focus is on atomic database transactions for request acceptance using `db.transaction()` to prevent duplicate assignments. I\'m building out features for active deliveries: implementing OTP verification at pickup, simulated GPS location updates, relay handover support with OTP authentication, and recipient OTP verification for delivery. I\'m preparing to integrate partner earnings tracking and payment settlement.\n\n\n**Integrating Earnings Dashboard**\n\nI\'m now integrating the earnings dashboard. I\'m focusing on features for partners, including earnings, deliveries (held/in-transit), completed payouts, and transaction history. I\'m building out a Recharts bar/area earnings chart for visual representation of earnings data.\n\n\n**Evaluating Safety Systems**\n\nI\'m now detailing safety features. I\'m dynamically calculating a reliability score from deliveries, and assessing on-time rates, cancellations, and compliance. I\'m building a suspicious activity detector to flag unusual bookings, high values, cancellations, prohibited content keywords, and weight/value mismatches. The \"MOVEX Guard\" will classify parcels as permitted, restricted, or prohibited. It is all designed to keep the system safe and secure.\n\n\n**Defining Core Architecture**\n\nNow I\'m designing key system components. I\'m focusing on the integration of a digital parcel identity, complete with a unique ID, QR code, and custody history. I am tracking 12 statuses, and building a disputes module. I\'m setting up payment and financial features, and planning an admin dashboard with key performance indicators, with tabs for overview, users, partners, parcels, and more.\n\n\n"
